{"name":"Ocean Boulevard","hex":"#a4c8c8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ocean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the large bodies of water separating the continents.","The continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ocean"},{"term":"boulevard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A broad, well-paved and landscaped thoroughfare.","The landscaping on the sides of a boulevard or other thoroughfare."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/boulevard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Florida State Road A1A","blocks":[{"text":"State Road A1A (SR A1A) is a major north–south Florida State Road consisting of seven separate sections running a total of 338.752 miles (545.168 km) along the Atlantic Ocean, from Key West at the southern tip of Florida, to Fernandina Beach, just south of Georgia on Amelia Island, before turning inland to Callahan. It is the main road through most oceanfront towns. Part of SR A1A between Flagler Beach and Jacksonville Beach is designated the A1A Scenic and Historic Coastal Byway, a National Scenic Byway. SR A1A is famous worldwide as a center of beach culture in the United States, a scenic coastal route through most Atlantic coastal cities and beach towns, including the unique tropical coral islands of the Florida Keys."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Florida_A1A.svg/330px-Florida_A1A.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Florida_State_Road_A1A"},{"title":"New Hampshire Route 1A","blocks":[{"text":"New Hampshire Route 1A is an 18.32-mile (29.48 km) long state highway located in southeast New Hampshire. The route runs along the Atlantic coastline from the Massachusetts border north to Rye, then turns toward downtown Portsmouth. The southern terminus is at the Massachusetts state line in Seabrook where it continues south as Massachusetts Route 1A. The northern terminus is at a junction with U.S. Route 1 in downtown Portsmouth. For the length of the road's run along the shore, its local name is Ocean Boulevard. In the northern part of Rye, it is known as Pioneer Road, and in Portsmouth, it is known as Miller Avenue and Sagamore Avenue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/NH_Route_1A.svg/330px-NH_Route_1A.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Hampshire_Route_1A"},{"title":"Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The ocean is the body of salt water that covers approximately 70.8% of Earth. The ocean is conventionally divided into large bodies of water, which are also referred to as oceans, and are themselves mostly divided into seas, gulfs, and subsequent bodies of water. The ocean contains 97% of Earth's water and is the primary component of Earth's hydrosphere, acting as a huge reservoir of heat for Earth's energy budget, as well as for its carbon cycle and water cycle, forming the basis for climate and weather patterns worldwide. The ocean is essential to life on Earth, harboring most of Earth's animals and protist life. It originated photosynthesis and therefore Earth's atmospheric oxygen, and still supplies half of it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg/330px-Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean"},{"title":"Boulevard","blocks":[{"text":"A boulevard is a type of broad avenue planted with rows of trees, or in parts of North America, any urban highway or wide road in a commercial district."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Park_Avenue_01.jpg/330px-Park_Avenue_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boulevard"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Beginning_of_A1A.JPG/500px-Beginning_of_A1A.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Beginning_of_A1A.JPG/1280px-Beginning_of_A1A.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Beginning_of_A1A.JPG/500px-Beginning_of_A1A.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The beginning of SR A1A (Milestone 0) at Bertha Street in Key West","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeginning_of_A1A.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/FL_A1A_NB_approaching_Harbour_Way_Bal_Harbour.jpeg/500px-FL_A1A_NB_approaching_Harbour_Way_Bal_Harbour.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/FL_A1A_NB_approaching_Harbour_Way_Bal_Harbour.jpeg/1280px-FL_A1A_NB_approaching_Harbour_Way_Bal_Harbour.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/FL_A1A_NB_approaching_Harbour_Way_Bal_Harbour.jpeg/500px-FL_A1A_NB_approaching_Harbour_Way_Bal_Harbour.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"SR A1A northbound in Bal Harbour","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFL_A1A_NB_approaching_Harbour_Way_Bal_Harbour.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/A1AnRoadEnd-US1nsUS23nsUS301ns-CallahanFL_%2832302420820%29.jpg/500px-A1AnRoadEnd-US1nsUS23nsUS301ns-CallahanFL_%2832302420820%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/A1AnRo